mers in the Murchison meteorite (https://www.nature.com/articles/28306)\n  45. The science from asteroid sample return missions (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2604.22182)\n  46. EXTRATERRESTRIAL AMINO ACIDS IDENTIFIED IN AN AGGREGATE SAMPLE RETURNED (https://www.hou.usra.edu/meetings/lpsc2024/pdf/1640.pdf)\n  47. Enantiomeric excess of amino acids in interstellar ice analogues-asymmetric photolysis of precursors by circularly polarized UV light (https://academic.oup.com/mnras/article/517/4/6112/6754748)\n  48. Photonenergy\u2010Controlled Symmetry Breaking with Circularly Polarized Light - Meinert - 2014 - Angewandte Chemie International Edition - Wiley Online Library (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/anie.201307855)\n  49. ENANTIOMERIC EXCESSES INDUCED IN AMINO ACIDS BY ULTRAVIOLET CIRCULARLY POLARIZED LIGHT IRRADIATION OF EXTRATERRESTRIAL ICE ANALOGS: A POSSIBLE SOURCE OF ASYMMETRY FOR PREBIOTIC CHEMISTRY - IOPscience (https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/0004-637X/788/1/79)\n  50. Anisotropy-Guided Enantiomeric Enhancement in AlanineUsing Far-UV Circularly Polarized Light (https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11084-015-9413-x)\n  51. Homochirality: a prerequisite or consequence of life? (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2012.12850)\n  52. NASA study reproduces origins of life on ocean floor (https://phys.org/news/2019-02-nasa-life-ocean-floor.html)\n\n=== END DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\n\n\n=== END LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS:\n1. Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the research above.\n2. Weigh the critiques and contested points the research reports; do not adopt a route uncritically.\n3. You may depart from the described routes when the chemistry justifies it; explain why in \"reasoning\".\n4. Cite the supporting paper (author, year) in each reaction's \"reasoning\" field, or mark the reaction as speculative.\n\n\nPREVIOUS FAILED ATTEMPTS - Learn from this feedback:\n============================================================\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.82\n- Feedback: This synthesis network for L-alanine covers the full range of prebiotic routes and explicitly acknowledges all major experimental and environmental constraints from the literature. Pathway diversity and environmental realism are strengths, as is the explicit enantioenrichment step\u2014a necessity since all feedstock routes yield racemic alanine. However, a majority of routes contain reactions that are not atom- or charge-balanced, which must be corrected for a fully valid network. The best pathway is extraterrestrial delivery (P9), as it is atomistically trivial and perfectly supported by sample data. Of the prebiotic synthesis routes, the Bucherer-Bergs path (P7) is the most immediately viable and least problematic. To reach 'excellent' status (>0.9 overall), upstream and aminating/reduction reactions must each be explicitly mass/charge balanced per the deterministic check. Overall, the network mirrors literature not only in route diversity but also in capturing the domain's main mechanistic caveats and is recommended with revision.\n- Issues: ['This pathway (CO2 \u2192 pyruvate \u2192 alanine via FeS/Fe(OH)2) is strongly aligned with literature and best understood experimentally. However, its mass balance is problematic: both rxn_001 (CO2 + H2 \u2192 pyruvate + acetate) and rxn_002 (pyruvate + NH3 \u2192 alanine + lactate) are systemically unbalanced in C, H, and O. As the structure is sound but reaction balancing is absent or faulty, the score is penalized for chemical feasibility. Hydrothermal conditions and mineral catalysts are appropriate and supported by experiment. The final chiral enrichment step is well-justified.', 'Hydrothermal CO2 \u2192 pyruvate \u2192 alanine via Ni0/H2 is a direct analog of recent experiments (Kaur et al.). Reaction conditions and catalysts are appropriate, but both rxn_001 and rxn_003 are unbalanced for C, H, and O. Network logic mirrors top-tier literature but is penalized for major mass conservation violations.', 'Hydrothermal transamination from pyruvate is plausible and experimentally supported (notably when pyridoxal and metal ions are present), but rxn_001 and rxn_010 reactions are not balanced. Otherwise, the logical steps and environmental assignments are robust. Chiral amplification step is correctly included.', \"Strecker/HCN photoredox cascade aligns with Sutherland's cyanosulfidic networks, but multiple steps are elementally unbalanced (notably rxn_007 and rxn_006). HCN photoreduction to acetaldehyde, as written, is not chemically balanced (and literature usually routes through intermediate sugars). The Strecker step is otherwise known and feasible. The pathway is realistic but must fix the stoichiometry.\", 'Classical Strecker synthesis from aldehyde atmospheres is textbook prebiotic chemistry. However, as shown, the key aminonitrile hydrolysis (rxn_006) is unbalanced, as is the aldehyde/HCN/NH3 condensation. Mechanistically sound but in need of explicit stoichiometry and byproduct tracking.', 'The Weber formose\u2013thiol pathway is experimentally shown. The formose condensation (rxn_008) is likely sound, but the thiol-assisted amination (rxn_009) is not atom-balanced (extra H, N, S). Further, the presence and prebiotic fate of 3-mercaptopropionic acid should be explicitly tracked. The path matches the literature but requires reaction balancing and attention to catalyst sources.', 'The pathway is plausible: formose reactions feed aldehydes into Strecker-type or ammonia condensation routes, but as written, rxn_005 and rxn_006 are unbalanced for atom numbers. The route matches credible scenarios (Kebukawa, Koga), especially for asteroidal/parent body settings, but technical improvements to balancing and intermediates are needed.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"L-Alanine\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"Ten converging, experimentally supported prebiotic routes to racemic alanine, finalized by a speculative L-enrichment module. Each route is explicitly atom-balanced for major species, tracks key byproducts, and adheres to network structure. Core nodes are (a) Strecker/HCN-aldehyde, (b) pyruvate reductive amination on Fe/Ni minerals, (c) Bucherer\u2013Bergs-type cyanide amination of keto acids, (d) parent-body and surface formose/sugar\u2013amination chemistries, and (e) extraterrestrial delivery, culminating in a plausible chiral amplification step. Each pathway captures realistic environmental and mineral requirements per the literature and addresses the mechanistic critiques reported.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"CO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"dihydrogen\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H3O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-oxopropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"pyruvic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H4O3/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h1H3,(H,5,6)\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"azane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2O\",\n  