de + glycolaldehyde to glyceraldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_012"], "outputs": ["mol_014"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["montmorillonite"], "conditions": {"temperature": "310-340K", "pressure": "ambient", "catalyst": "clay minerals", "aqueous": true, "other": "wet-dry cycling favored"}, "mechanism": "Aldol-type condensation of formaldehyde and glycolaldehyde yields higher sugars, including glyceraldehyde.", "reasoning": "Well established as the surface formose reaction (Weber 1998, Kebukawa 2017). Mass balance includes water removal and tracking of single C3 product for this route."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Thiol-catalyzed amination of glyceraldehyde to alanine/lactate", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_004", "mol_013"], "outputs": ["mol_006", "mol_007", "mol_005", "mol_013"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["3-mercaptopropionic acid (thiol)"], "conditions": {"temperature": "315-320K", "pressure": "ambient", "catalyst": "thiol", "aqueous": true, "other": "slightly acidic, pH 5\u20135.5"}, "mechanism": "Glyceraldehyde forms hemithioacetal, then imine with NH3, then yields alanine (plus lactate as alternative product), thiol recycled.", "reasoning": "Weber (1985, J. Mol. Evol.; 1998, OLEB): C3 sugar chemistry to alanine, thiol-catalyzed, atom balance includes thiol cycling."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Bucherer\u2013Bergs: Pyruvate + cyanide + ammonia + CO2 to hydantoin", "inputs": ["mol_003", "mol_009", "mol_004", "mol_001", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_016", "mol_005"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": [null], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "neutral to slightly basic, CO2 required"}, "mechanism": "Pyruvate, ammonia, cyanide, and CO2 condense and cyclize to form hydantoin, with water release.", "reasoning": "Documented by Pulletikurti et al. (2022, Nature Chem.): atom balance checked, CO2 required as per experiment."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Hydantoin hydrolysis to L-alanine", "inputs": ["mol_016", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_006", "mol_001"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": [null], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-350K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "hydrolytic conditions"}, "mechanism": "Hydantoin ring opens and hydrolyzes to alanine, CO2 released.", "reasoning": "Classic Bucherer\u2013Bergs amination route (Pulletikurti et al. 2022); atom balance verified."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Reductive amination of pyruvate with hydroxylamine on Fe0", "inputs": ["mol_003", "mol_018"], "outputs": ["mol_006", "mol_007"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["native Fe0"], "conditions": {"temperature": "298-320K", "pressure": "moderate", "catalyst": "Fe0", "aqueous": true, "other": "mildly acidic to neutral"}, "mechanism": "Pyruvate forms an oxime with hydroxylamine, then is reduced by Fe0 to alanine and competing lactate.", "reasoning": "Muchowska et al. (2019, Nature); Fe0 as reductant, yields multiple amino acids, atom balance included."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Transamination: Aspartate + pyruvate yields alanine", "inputs": ["mol_019", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_006", "mol_001"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["pyridoxal", "Fe(III)", "Al(III)"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-350K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "pyridoxal, metal ions", "aqueous": true, "other": "neutral to alkaline"}, "mechanism": "Amino transfer from aspartate to pyruvate (pyridoxal catalyzed) generates alanine and CO2.", "reasoning": "Dherbassy et al. (2023, JACS): metal-assisted transamination, atom balance (see Prebiotic Synthesis of Aspartate, Life 2023)."}, {"id": "rxn_013", "name": "Extraterrestrial delivery of racemic alanine", "inputs": [], "outputs": ["mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [null], "conditions": {"temperature": "variable", "pressure": "variable", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": false, "other": "delivery via meteorites/asteroids"}, "mechanism": "Alanine synthesized in parent-body (asteroidal/interstellar) chemistry, delivered intact to the surface.", "reasoning": "Strongly supported by meteorite and asteroid return studies: Murchison (Engel & Macko, 1997; Pizzarello & Cronin, 1998), Bennu (Glavin et al. 2025).\nAtom balance is trivial since alanine is delivered as is."}, {"id": "rxn_014", "name": "Chiral enrichment of L-alanine via asymmetric photolysis/amplification", "inputs": ["mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["circularly polarized UV light", "chiral mineral (speculative)"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320K", "pressure": "ambient", "catalyst": "possible chiral mineral, otherwise none", "aqueous": false, "other": "requires chiral amplification, not demonstrated in experiment"}, "mechanism": "Partial destruction of D-alanine by CPL or chiral-interface crystallization increases L-alanine fraction after racemic synthesis.", "reasoning": "Speculative, in accordance with Meinert et al. (2014), Modica (2014), Glavin (2025) on amplification; no prebiotic scenario fully solves the 'L' problem. Included as a necessary but unresolved step."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "CO2 hydrothermal reduction and iron-catalyzed amination", "description": "CO2 and H2 react to form pyruvate, which is aminated by Fe hydroxide green rust and ammonia to give racemic alanine, with a speculative final L-amplification.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_014"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_003"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "CO2 hydrothermal reduction and Ni(H2)-catalyzed amination", "description": "CO2 and H2 make pyruvate via Fe-Ni minerals, then pyruvate is aminated by Ni0 with H2 and NH3 to alanine, followed by optional enrichment.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_014"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_003"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "Pyruvate reduction via FeS to lactate/alanine (competing)", "description": "CO2/H2 yields pyruvate; pyruvate is reduced to lactate or alanine on FeS, modeling an ancient competing sink (informs pathway efficiency).", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_003"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_003"]}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "Strecker cyanosulfidic photochemistry (HCN from surface)", "description": "Aldehydes from glycolaldehyde, aminated by HCN and ammonia (Strecker), then hydrolyzed to alanine and enriched by CPL/later amplification.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_014"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_008", "mol_010"]}, {"pathway_id": "P5", "name": "Surface formose + Strecker (alternate aldehyde supply)", "description": "Surface formaldehyde and glycolaldehyde via formose gives glycolaldehyde, which is converted to acetaldehyde for Strecker amination.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_007", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_014"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_014", "mol_008", "mol_010"]}, {"pathway_id": "P6", "name": "Formose\u2013thiol amination (Weber chemistry)", "description": "Formose sugars (glyceraldehyde) undergo thiol-catalyzed amination with ammonia, giving alanine plus lactate as minor product, followed by photolytic chiral amplification.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_014"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_014"]}, {"pathway_id": "P7", "name": "Bucherer\u2013Bergs amination of hydrothermal pyruvate", "description": "Hydrothermally formed pyruvate, cyanide, and ammonia plus CO2 assemble into hydantoin; hydrolysis yields alanine, followed by chiral enrichment.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_009", "rxn_010", "rxn_014"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_003", "mol_016"]}, {"pathway_id": "P8", "name": "Fe0/hydroxylamine amination (alternative pyruvate utilization)", "description": "Hydrothermal pyruvate reacts with hydroxylamine on Fe0 to give alanine and lactate, enabling prebiotic amination by nonammonia nitrogen.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_011", "rxn_014"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_003"]}, {"pathway_id": "P9", "